An exam.

A student has a name (text) and marks (a list of whole numbers).

The average of a student (a student) is a number:
the sum of its marks divided by the length of its marks rounded to 1 places.

A student (a student) passes when:
the average of the student is at least 50 and the mark is at least 30 for every mark in the student's marks.

The passing names in a class (a list of students) is a list of texts:
its name for each student in the class where the student passes.

Suppose ana is a student with name "Ana" and marks a list of 70, 80 and 60.
Suppose bo is a student with name "Bo" and marks a list of 90, 95 and 20.
Suppose cy is a student with name "Cy" and marks a list of 40, 45 and 50.
For example, the average of ana is 70.
For example, ana passes.
For example, bo passes is false.
For example, cy passes is false.
For example, the passing names in a list of ana, bo and cy joined with "," is "Ana".
